  "reasoning": "The patient presents with fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, weakness, and fatigue. These symptoms collectively point towards a systemic viral infection affecting the upper respiratory tract. While malaria and dengue cause fever and fatigue, they are distinguished by severe body/joint aches, rashes, or pain behind the eyes. Allergies typically lack fever. The presence of swollen lymph nodes alongside fever and generalized weakness is a hallmark of a common cold or similar viral illness. Therefore, common cold is the most appropriate diagnosis from the given list.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]common cold[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}

{
  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a systemic febrile illness characterized by fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, significant weakness, fatigue, and difficulty concentrating. Evaluating the available options: respiratory conditions (common cold, pneumonia, bronchial asthma) typically feature cough, congestion, or breathing difficulties, which are absent here. Gastrointestinal and urinary conditions (typhoid, peptic ulcer disease, GERD, UTI) lack corresponding abdominal, digestive, or urinary symptoms. Dermatological, allergic, and metabolic conditions (psoriasis, fungal infection, impetigo, chicken pox, drug reaction, allergy, diabetes, jaundice) do not match the absence of rashes, itching, sneezing, or metabolic signs. Neurological and vascular conditions (migraine, hypertension, cervical spondylosis, varicose veins, arthritis) are ruled out by the acute febrile nature and lack of specific focal symptoms. Malaria classically presents with high fever, severe headache, profound weakness, fatigue, and systemic inflammatory responses that can include lymph node swelling. Given the symptom cluster, malaria is the most consistent diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]malaria[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
